export const body = "<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>1) Collaborative decision-making</li><li>2) Never wait!</li><li>3) In-depth style guide</li><li>Nurturing the end-to-end mindset</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n\n<p>So far, our teams have helped refine the art style and establish the 3D look for the game. On the technical front, they have been providing workflow and pipeline support to the client. In short, our teams are deeply integrated into the creation of the project (just what we call: End-to-end development).</p>\n\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f982502aeaf9716e5b08.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f982502aeaf9716e5b08.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"458\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>1) Collaborative decision-making</h3>\n\n<p><strong>How would you describe your relationship with the client?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>I would say we're an extension of the client, like an adaptive exoskeleton suit to a human body. My team members are different components on that suit, powering and filling the gaps of the client's pipeline. And because we are one interconnected unit, we can coordinate internally to complete a diverse range of tasks for them, instead of individual bits and pieces. When the client's response is delayed, we're still able to solve imminent problems by implementing our basic emergency measures.</p>\n\n<p>Traditionally, outsourcing teams are like independent accessories with specific functions. These accessories work separately without communication and cooperation. They cannot take initiative to make offerings to the client. They fully rely on the body to tell them what to do.</p>\n\n<p>Both systems can accomplish the same goals, but the \"exoskeleton\" model can provide clients with more flexibility and reduce management costs. Of course, in any case, the “exoskeleton” still obeys the command and control of the “body.\"</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e000f2df68aaa78d8ade.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e000f2df68aaa78d8ade.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"252\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Can you share with us how that relationship manifested in this project?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>One time, when the project just started, the client only had a rough project briefing, and they wanted to kick off production as quickly as possible. They wanted to use dynamic lighting instead of static lighting. But it was clear that the workflow of this stage wouldn't be reused in mass production later. After analyzing their prototype levels, that decision sparked plenty of questions in our minds, like:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Which specific dynamic lighting techniques do they plan to use?</li><li>Do they have the necessary master shaders?</li><li>Do they support translucency or transparency?</li><li>How do we use the standard review environment of these lighting and materials?</li><li>How about the textures? Full tilling or full unique? Or a combination of both?</li><li>How can we further optimize the production method?</li></ul>\n\n<p>We knew many aspects of the project weren't final yet, so we welcomed the uncertainty. As an Art Director, I worked with my TLs and TAs to compile a list which we called \"Technique specification Q&amp;A.\" It contained our questions and concerns, as well as images to illustrate our points.</p>\n\n<p>After the client received that list, they opened some internal discussions before writing to answer our questions. Upon receiving their answers, we tried to approach things per their suggestion. If something didn't work, we would tell them, giving them further opinions and suggestions, so that they could have more options to choose from. Our relationship with the client had become an interactive R&amp;D cycle.</p>\n\n<p><strong>That's an impressive feat of collaboration! What does that Q&amp;A file look like?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>It's like an Excel table with columns to list the target platforms and the client's response, and many rows to list the components that I needed their input on. This table is editable by everyone in my team, so I always encourage them to raise their questions or concerns.</p>\n\n<p><em>After the interview, we made a sample based on the artist’s description. Have a look!</em><em><br></em></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td></td><td><p><strong>Platform 1</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Platform 2</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Platform 3</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>The client's answers</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Lighting</strong></p></td><td><p>Here, we think baked lighting is key. But how do we handle the procedural levels?</p></td><td><p>With the extra hardware power, this platform can handle dynamic lighting.</p></td><td><p>We plan to use raytracing in combination with DLSS here.</p></td><td><p>A lot of content in our levels are procedurally generated, so we should focus on dynamic lighting for now.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Level streaming</strong></p></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>LOD</strong></p></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Texture</strong></p></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em></em><em>However, after the release, we received confirmation from the artist that the file can only be shared to the people who work on the project.</em></p>\n<p><em>We are sincerely sorry for this mistake.</em></p></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Question: How does your team ask questions or make suggestions to the client?<br></p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>2) Never wait!</h3>\n\n<p><strong>We can tell that the client trusts the team a lot. What is the key to this level of trust?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>The ar</strong>tist and his team <strong>never stalled for direction</strong> from the client. They constantly analyzed the feedback and extracted the rules themselves. Instead of staying idle during feedback cycles, they actively seek probable solutions to later present to the client. Besides, they are very open to change and having their ideas turned down. They would be like, \"I didn't know what you wanted, so I tried something else.\" This mentality is key to building rapport and gaining trust from clients.</p>\n\n<p><strong>We assume communication played a huge role?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>Communication was very important because we wanted to let the client know that: \"We are in this together. Your bottlenecks are our bottlenecks. So if we face them, we'll push through together.\" Therefore, when the client seemed unsure about something, we shared our approach with them so that they would have more options to consider. And when they weren't facing any trouble, we made sure they were always aware of our direction. So we kept informing them of:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Where we are in the progress</li><li>What we are doing</li><li>We let them know what the AD is thinking for the next step</li><li>We share screenshots of our progress (of an asset or a level) with the client</li><li>We update our P4 with detailed changelogs</li></ul>\n\n<p>Basically, anything that is visible to us, is visible to the client.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9cccaf5d289753d7e7f3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9cccaf5d289753d7e7f3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"236\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Question: How does your team take advantage of idle time?<br></p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>3) In-depth style guide</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Can you tell us about the concept pipeline?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>For this project, we established a process for 3D and concept to work together.</p>\n\n<ul><li>The client provides mood concepts</li><li>the two artists work with the client, and delivers POI without going into details on assets</li><li>team produces asset lists and art briefs</li><li>Art Brief Specialists identify what assets need further concepts or references</li><li>CD concept team does detailed asset concepts when necessary</li></ul>\n\n<p>The asset concept artist will paint the design based on the whitebox provided by the 3D art team. They had to ensure the asset's structure meet the 3D requirements while improving on the visual details. Then, the concept will be delivered to level artist to see whether or not the paint-over makes sense.</p>\n\n<p><strong>How did you and your team pick up the art style?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>When the client first came to us, they only had a high-level art bible, which wasn't final. I knew, for mass production, my team would need a more in-depth version. Since the content was underdeveloped, I'd decided to build a visual framework that my team could reference to analyze the style appeal, definition, and application.</p>\n\n<p>So, I wrote a project style analysis report based on the client's art bible and other related materials. I combed their materials and conducted a personal read on the game's art style. Then, I compared them with other games on the market to point out the potential style direction. I also proposed a concept design framework to connect the client's style with the actual design content required for 3D production. Finally, I compiled some questions and sent them to the client. After the client gave more specific information, we converted it into several benchmark assets, then returned it to the client for review and selection.</p>\n\n<p>My goal was to help my team gain deep understanding of the art style so that they could work without much external input, whether it is me or the client.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a2515ef7516a80d2e6e6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a2515ef7516a80d2e6e6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"429\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><br></p>\n\n<p><strong> How long did it take you to do all of that?</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>It took me about 2 weeks. You can add a couple of days if you count the follow-up verification of technical solutions and specification formulation. The total time investment is comparatively small, but the long-term benefits are great.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><em>Below is the structure of the style guide made by </em><em>the artist.</em><em><br></em></p></div>\n\n<p>1</p>\n\n<p><strong>Overview</strong></p>\n\n<ul><li>Time logs: dates of updates</li><li>Topics of discussion</li></ul>\n\n<p>2</p>\n\n<p><strong>Style description</strong></p>\n\n<ul><li>Degree of stylization in comparison with other games</li><li>Simplification of color usage and shape language</li><li>Analysis of the client's prototype design</li></ul>\n\n<p>3</p>\n\n<p><strong>Visual framework</strong></p>\n\n<ul><li>Approval contact list: stages and respective owners</li><li>Look-dev process</li><li>Graphic design elements</li><li>Color palettes</li><li>Material design</li><li>3D shape language</li><li>Modular asset guidelines</li></ul>\n\n<p>4</p>\n\n<p><strong>General schedule</strong></p>\n\n<ul><li>Overview of the schedule</li><li>\"Where we are\"</li><li>\"Where we need to arrive at\"</li><li>\"What we can do to get there\"</li></ul>\n\n<p><strong>Overall, how do you feel about this experience?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong></strong>We feel a kind of exciting pressure! And we are not at a loss. We can see every little change result in a series of changes, even to the estimates. We can see people of all levels support and coordinate so well with one another. So, overall, this experience is a net positive. Though the pressure is double, our satisfaction is immeasurable.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/043007e559f884d1cd66.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/043007e559f884d1cd66.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"348\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Question: Does your team receive detailed art bible from the client? Or you have to elaborate on an existing art bible?<br></p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>Nurturing the end-to-end mindset</h3>\n\n<p>Perhaps, our biggest takeaway from talking to the artist is the importance of the end-to-end mindset.</p>\n\n<p>What tips do you have for teams that are moving toward the end-to-end model?</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Question: Of these 3 measures, which one are you going to pick up?<br></p>\n\n<p>ThemesCollaborative decision-makingNever wait!In-depth style guideAll of them!</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/043007e559f884d1cd66.webp", "assets/library-inline/9cccaf5d289753d7e7f3.webp", "assets/library-inline/a2515ef7516a80d2e6e6.webp", "assets/library-inline/e000f2df68aaa78d8ade.webp", "assets/library-inline/f982502aeaf9716e5b08.webp"];
